1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,600
that you can take advantage of.

2
00:00:03,920 --> 00:00:07,340
All right, so questions about mean reversion?

3
00:00:07,340 --> 00:00:09,180
So again, mean reversion is one of these ideas

4
00:00:09,180 --> 00:00:11,920
where if it's true, it is a way

5
00:00:11,920 --> 00:00:13,700
to probabilistically beat the market,

6
00:00:13,700 --> 00:00:16,380
although of course, the main thing

7
00:00:16,380 --> 00:00:19,180
with any of these anomalies is realize

8
00:00:19,180 --> 00:00:20,700
that just because it exists statistically

9
00:00:20,700 --> 00:00:25,040
doesn't mean that it's big or that it's in any way certain

10
00:00:25,040 --> 00:00:27,300
or that it's ideal.

11
00:00:27,300 --> 00:00:28,780
Yeah, so there's a famous lawsuit

12
00:00:28,780 --> 00:00:31,420
where University of Chicago Business School professor

13
00:00:31,420 --> 00:00:32,900
told a class of business students,

14
00:00:32,900 --> 00:00:37,780
I guarantee this equation will never screw an investor.

15
00:00:37,780 --> 00:00:39,100
And then one of the students used it

16
00:00:39,100 --> 00:00:40,220
and was screwed by the equation

17
00:00:40,220 --> 00:00:42,900
and then he took the professor to court for that.

18
00:00:42,900 --> 00:00:44,500
And the court had a very important

19
00:00:44,500 --> 00:00:47,220
and extremely wise ruling,

20
00:00:47,220 --> 00:00:50,100
which says that no professor can ever be held liable

21
00:00:50,100 --> 00:00:51,340
for anything he says.

22
00:00:51,340 --> 00:00:52,180
All right.

23
00:00:52,180 --> 00:00:55,480
Perfect, essentially.

24
00:00:55,480 --> 00:00:59,220
Right, so George Stigler had a very amused comment on this

25
00:00:59,220 --> 00:01:02,620
on how isn't it surprising that people

26
00:01:02,620 --> 00:01:06,060
who are very close to the professorial community,

27
00:01:06,060 --> 00:01:08,180
namely judges, come up with a ruling

28
00:01:08,180 --> 00:01:11,260
which shields professors from any responsibility

29
00:01:11,260 --> 00:01:12,620
for anything they ever say.

30
00:01:14,340 --> 00:01:15,300
All right.

31
00:01:15,300 --> 00:01:17,260
Of course, if you believe the Ecker-Lavellev model,

32
00:01:17,260 --> 00:01:20,660
then we might wanna be suable for what we say, right?

33
00:01:20,660 --> 00:01:22,100
What's the advantage in being suable

34
00:01:22,100 --> 00:01:23,560
for wrong things you say?

35
00:01:23,560 --> 00:01:25,560
There's an advantage.

36
00:01:25,560 --> 00:01:27,800
Compensated for the right things you say.

37
00:01:27,800 --> 00:01:30,040
Yes, well, people will listen to you.

38
00:01:30,040 --> 00:01:32,160
People will care what you say.

39
00:01:32,160 --> 00:01:34,760
People will pay you more for your advice.

40
00:01:34,760 --> 00:01:39,040
Whereas if it is known that there is no liability whatsoever

41
00:01:39,040 --> 00:01:41,640
then people don't care as much.

42
00:01:43,120 --> 00:01:44,360
Okay, all right.

43
00:01:44,360 --> 00:01:47,600
So questions about mean reversion?

44
00:01:47,600 --> 00:01:48,920
I'm happy.

45
00:01:48,920 --> 00:01:51,440
All right, let's move on to betting market anomalies.

46
00:01:51,440 --> 00:01:52,640
So remember back in week two,

47
00:01:52,640 --> 00:01:54,600
when we talked about betting markets

48
00:01:54,600 --> 00:01:57,020
and how Robin Hanson is one of the world's leading

49
00:01:57,020 --> 00:01:58,800
researchers on betting markets,

50
00:01:58,800 --> 00:02:00,720
including betting on terrorism

51
00:02:00,720 --> 00:02:02,960
and other controversial things,

52
00:02:02,960 --> 00:02:06,840
which do not actually plausibly cause terrorism

53
00:02:06,840 --> 00:02:08,980
because, well, many reasons,

54
00:02:08,980 --> 00:02:11,440
but one is the maximum bet was 100 bucks.

55
00:02:11,440 --> 00:02:12,880
So you have to imagine there's someone

56
00:02:12,880 --> 00:02:14,280
who wasn't gonna kill themselves

57
00:02:14,280 --> 00:02:15,800
and then, oh, there's 100 bucks in it.

58
00:02:15,800 --> 00:02:17,920
And then the other one is that the betting market

59
00:02:17,920 --> 00:02:20,400
is monitored by the CIA.

60
00:02:20,400 --> 00:02:23,600
So you'd have to go and do this

61
00:02:23,600 --> 00:02:25,480
and then withstand scrutiny by the CIA.

62
00:02:25,480 --> 00:02:27,880
So that's also, other than in order to get paid.

63
00:02:27,880 --> 00:02:29,880
So it's also not too plausible.

64
00:02:29,880 --> 00:02:32,080
Anyway, so betting markets are just a special kind

65
00:02:32,080 --> 00:02:33,660
of asset market.

66
00:02:33,660 --> 00:02:35,600
So if I hold up a piece of paper

67
00:02:35,600 --> 00:02:37,800
that says I will pay you a dollar

68
00:02:37,800 --> 00:02:40,760
if inflation goes over 10% in the next 10 years,

69
00:02:40,760 --> 00:02:43,480
that's really, like, that's really an asset, right?

70
00:02:43,480 --> 00:02:45,560
That's an asset that pays under certain conditions.

71
00:02:45,560 --> 00:02:47,100
We can call it a bet or an asset.

72
00:02:47,100 --> 00:02:49,360
Really just a bet is a certain kind of asset.

73
00:02:49,360 --> 00:02:51,400
That's all it is, all right?

74
00:02:51,400 --> 00:02:53,480
And then the same empirical techniques

75
00:02:53,480 --> 00:02:55,880
that we apply to other asset markets, right?

76
00:02:55,880 --> 00:02:58,040
And really the same equation over here.

77
00:02:58,040 --> 00:02:59,560
Could be, you could go and take it

78
00:02:59,560 --> 00:03:02,040
and apply it to betting markets just as well.

79
00:03:02,040 --> 00:03:04,320
Apply it to betting markets just as well.

80
00:03:04,320 --> 00:03:06,860
So, and a lot of the things that we see in betting markets

81
00:03:06,860 --> 00:03:08,480
is just the way they expect.

82
00:03:08,480 --> 00:03:10,520
So for one thing, there's a really high correlation

83
00:03:10,520 --> 00:03:12,720
between subjective and objective probabilities

84
00:03:12,720 --> 00:03:14,460
in betting markets.

85
00:03:14,460 --> 00:03:16,520
So I think I was already showing you

86
00:03:16,520 --> 00:03:18,600
what calibration looks like.

87
00:03:18,600 --> 00:03:21,880
We're showing you in, so when people go and look at bets,

88
00:03:21,880 --> 00:03:23,480
and here we've got

89
00:03:26,600 --> 00:03:29,480
subjective probabilities as reflected in,

90
00:03:29,480 --> 00:03:32,040
like, the initial prices that bets go for.

91
00:03:32,040 --> 00:03:34,560
And then we've got objective probabilities,

92
00:03:35,680 --> 00:03:39,440
which is the actual frequency with which different bets win.

93
00:03:39,440 --> 00:03:42,800
So again, if we start, so we start at, like, 0.5 to one,

94
00:03:42,800 --> 00:03:46,480
then we are quite close to a nice 45 degree line

95
00:03:46,480 --> 00:03:50,560
where if a bet is going for 50% of its face value,

96
00:03:50,560 --> 00:03:52,760
then it generally actually wins 60% of the time.

97
00:03:52,760 --> 00:03:56,080
If it's going for, say, 80% of its face value,

98
00:03:56,080 --> 00:03:58,280
wins about 80% of the time.

99
00:03:58,280 --> 00:04:00,000
And again, this is a price,

100
00:04:00,000 --> 00:04:02,760
and this is a price before things happen,

101
00:04:02,760 --> 00:04:04,480
and that's a frequency after things happen,

102
00:04:04,480 --> 00:04:07,120
and we can then go and plop them together.

103
00:04:07,120 --> 00:04:08,760
And again, of course, it's possible,

104
00:04:08,760 --> 00:04:09,800
like if people are really dumb,

105
00:04:09,800 --> 00:04:10,980
if you're betting against two-year-olds

106
00:04:10,980 --> 00:04:12,760
or something like that, not that you should,

107
00:04:12,760 --> 00:04:14,700
but if you're betting against two-year-olds,

108
00:04:14,700 --> 00:04:17,600
then maybe you would see that the line would look like that,

109
00:04:17,600 --> 00:04:18,440
or it could look weird,

110
00:04:18,440 --> 00:04:20,120
or there would go all kinds of weird lines

111
00:04:20,120 --> 00:04:22,520
if you're betting against people who are just clueless.

112
00:04:22,520 --> 00:04:24,440
But these, remember, these are subjective probabilities,

113
00:04:24,440 --> 00:04:25,440
but they're market prices,

114
00:04:25,440 --> 00:04:27,440
so they are the subjective probability

115
00:04:27,440 --> 00:04:28,600
of the marginal bettor.

116
00:04:29,760 --> 00:04:30,920
So it's not even average,

117
00:04:30,920 --> 00:04:34,160
it's like what the marginal person's willing to do.

118
00:04:34,160 --> 00:04:35,680
Okay.

119
00:04:35,680 --> 00:04:36,800
All right.

120
00:04:36,800 --> 00:04:39,360
So, so anyway, so that is,

121
00:04:39,360 --> 00:04:40,680
describes a lot of what's going on.

122
00:04:40,680 --> 00:04:43,940
So betting markets do very well by most tests,

123
00:04:43,940 --> 00:04:46,240
but there are a number of anomalies.

124
00:04:46,240 --> 00:04:47,480
All right.

125
00:04:47,480 --> 00:04:51,240
So, and of course, once you start looking for anomalies,

126
00:04:51,240 --> 00:04:52,840
eventually you find some.

127
00:04:52,840 --> 00:04:55,480
So probably the very best known betting market anomaly

128
00:04:55,480 --> 00:04:56,920
is called long shot bias.

129
00:04:59,760 --> 00:05:00,740
Long shot bias.

130
00:05:02,520 --> 00:05:03,360
All right.

131
00:05:03,360 --> 00:05:05,600
Generally demonstrated by studying horse racing.

132
00:05:06,880 --> 00:05:08,840
All right, now if you ever wondered how it is

133
00:05:08,840 --> 00:05:12,120
that horse tracks make sure they'll have enough money,

134
00:05:12,120 --> 00:05:13,740
the answer is they actually have a procedure

135
00:05:13,740 --> 00:05:15,480
that can't fail,

136
00:05:15,480 --> 00:05:17,720
which is if, say, people are betting three times

137
00:05:17,720 --> 00:05:19,800
as much money on one horse than another,

138
00:05:19,800 --> 00:05:22,040
then they actually use that to say,

139
00:05:22,040 --> 00:05:24,160
in that case, there are three to one odds.

140
00:05:24,160 --> 00:05:25,760
And that way, and so, you know,

141
00:05:25,760 --> 00:05:27,760
if there's three times as much money put in favor of horse,

142
00:05:27,760 --> 00:05:28,960
put in favor of one horse,

143
00:05:28,960 --> 00:05:32,560
then if the one where there's three times as much money wins,

144
00:05:32,560 --> 00:05:34,080
you only get a small payoff,

145
00:05:34,080 --> 00:05:35,640
paid for by the,

146
00:05:38,640 --> 00:05:42,280
three to one, so by the 25% of people bet the other way.

147
00:05:42,280 --> 00:05:44,600
So like the winners all get an equal share

148
00:05:44,600 --> 00:05:46,840
of the 25% of money that went the other way,

149
00:05:46,840 --> 00:05:49,360
but if the unpopular thing wins,

150
00:05:49,360 --> 00:05:50,920
then they each get to triple their money,

151
00:05:50,920 --> 00:05:53,160
minus transaction costs, of course.

152
00:05:53,160 --> 00:05:55,720
Right, so, you know, so if there are,

153
00:05:55,720 --> 00:05:58,040
you know, there's three times as much money bet on,

154
00:05:58,040 --> 00:06:00,240
you know, like, I don't know,

155
00:06:00,240 --> 00:06:01,800
what's a good name of a horse?

156
00:06:03,320 --> 00:06:04,160
Sure, Shadowfax.

157
00:06:04,160 --> 00:06:05,880
Shadowfax is one horse.

158
00:06:05,880 --> 00:06:07,760
And what's your horse?

159
00:06:07,760 --> 00:06:08,760
She always wins.

160
00:06:08,760 --> 00:06:10,000
Yeah, she always wins with the other horse.

161
00:06:10,000 --> 00:06:12,280
So three times as much money is put on Shadowfax,

162
00:06:12,280 --> 00:06:13,760
then she always wins.

163
00:06:13,760 --> 00:06:16,700
Then if you go and bet on Shadowfax and you win,

164
00:06:18,440 --> 00:06:21,000
you're going to get a one to three payoff.

165
00:06:21,000 --> 00:06:23,000
So like for every dollar, for every $3 you put in,

166
00:06:23,000 --> 00:06:26,400
you get $1 back, which means that the bets on

167
00:06:26,400 --> 00:06:29,720
she always wins are gonna be enough to cover those bets.

168
00:06:29,720 --> 00:06:32,280
On the other hand, if she always wins wins,

169
00:06:32,280 --> 00:06:35,880
or then, every person who bet a dollar there

170
00:06:35,880 --> 00:06:37,920
gets $3 from people the other way.

171
00:06:37,920 --> 00:06:39,640
So this is called parimutuel betting.

172
00:06:39,640 --> 00:06:43,680
It's a very simple way to make sure that you're solvent,

173
00:06:43,680 --> 00:06:45,480
because you set the odds just based upon

174
00:06:45,480 --> 00:06:47,080
the total volume of dollars that people

175
00:06:47,080 --> 00:06:49,280
are putting down on every possibility.

176
00:06:49,280 --> 00:06:50,120
All right.

177
00:06:50,920 --> 00:06:54,720
So, normally this works fine,

178
00:06:54,720 --> 00:06:56,920
but one thing that's been noticed is that

179
00:06:56,920 --> 00:06:58,640
there is long shot bias, which says that

180
00:06:58,640 --> 00:07:00,520
expected returns in horse racing

181
00:07:00,520 --> 00:07:03,280
increase with the probability of the horse winning.

182
00:07:03,280 --> 00:07:05,420
So in other words, this says that if you bet on the horse

183
00:07:05,420 --> 00:07:06,880
that is favored to win, say one horse

184
00:07:06,880 --> 00:07:08,560
has an 80% chance of winning,

185
00:07:08,560 --> 00:07:11,440
you generally get a higher payoff per dollar

186
00:07:11,440 --> 00:07:12,640
than if you bet on the long shot,

187
00:07:12,640 --> 00:07:15,440
the horse that only has a one in 30 chance of winning.

188
00:07:15,440 --> 00:07:18,240
Now again, notice this isn't the obvious thing that

189
00:07:18,240 --> 00:07:19,880
the total number of dollars that you get

190
00:07:19,880 --> 00:07:21,040
when you bet on a favored horse

191
00:07:21,040 --> 00:07:22,680
are less than the total number that you win

192
00:07:22,680 --> 00:07:25,560
than if you bet on a unfavored horse and you win.

193
00:07:25,560 --> 00:07:30,560
This is saying that the actual expected payoff is lower.

194
00:07:30,680 --> 00:07:32,000
The expected payoff is actually lower,

195
00:07:32,000 --> 00:07:33,400
whereas in that example that I gave you,

196
00:07:33,400 --> 00:07:35,120
the expected payoffs are equal.

197
00:07:35,120 --> 00:07:38,240
Right, so either you have a three quarter chance of winning,

198
00:07:38,240 --> 00:07:41,120
but if you win, you only get one third of a dollar,

199
00:07:41,120 --> 00:07:42,920
or you have a 25% chance of winning,

200
00:07:42,920 --> 00:07:45,000
but if you do win, you get $3 for every dollar.

201
00:07:45,000 --> 00:07:48,120
So that means the two bets are actually equal in value.

202
00:07:48,120 --> 00:07:50,160
But the long shot bias says that

203
00:07:50,160 --> 00:07:51,360
if you go and bet on the safe bets,

204
00:07:51,360 --> 00:07:54,420
you actually on average take home more money in the end.

205
00:07:54,420 --> 00:07:55,860
And if you go and bet on the long shots,

206
00:07:55,860 --> 00:07:58,160
you take home less money in the end.

207
00:07:58,160 --> 00:08:01,720
All right, now one explanation that

208
00:08:01,720 --> 00:08:03,560
lifelong gamblers generally have,

209
00:08:03,560 --> 00:08:05,720
and which people who research it

210
00:08:05,720 --> 00:08:08,640
have said seems plausible anyway,

211
00:08:08,640 --> 00:08:11,960
is that a lot of people actually want to leave

212
00:08:12,840 --> 00:08:15,240
either in the money or at least not out of the money.

213
00:08:15,240 --> 00:08:19,000
So basically they don't want to leave having lost any money.

214
00:08:19,000 --> 00:08:22,140
All right, now of course since there is a commission

215
00:08:22,140 --> 00:08:24,520
or a management fee deducted for every bet,

216
00:08:24,520 --> 00:08:27,000
on average people are losing money every time, right?

217
00:08:27,000 --> 00:08:29,300
So if at the end of every day,

218
00:08:29,300 --> 00:08:31,640
typically you will have lost money.

219
00:08:31,640 --> 00:08:35,560
And if then you want to try to avoid losing any money,

220
00:08:35,560 --> 00:08:37,600
then at the end of the day you will go

221
00:08:37,600 --> 00:08:38,620
and bet on the long shot,

222
00:08:38,620 --> 00:08:40,560
which is your only way of making it up.

223
00:08:41,440 --> 00:08:43,880
And if there are enough people doing this,

224
00:08:43,880 --> 00:08:46,160
regardless of the actual objective odds

225
00:08:46,160 --> 00:08:47,840
of the long shot horses winning,

226
00:08:47,840 --> 00:08:48,920
there will be too much money

227
00:08:48,920 --> 00:08:51,120
poured into the long shot horses,

228
00:08:51,120 --> 00:08:52,720
which will mean that the odds,

229
00:08:52,720 --> 00:08:55,000
although the odds are still good,

230
00:08:55,000 --> 00:08:56,320
the payoffs are still good,

231
00:08:56,320 --> 00:08:57,920
they're not as good as they should be.

232
00:08:57,920 --> 00:09:01,640
So basically a horse that has a 30 to one odds to win,

233
00:09:01,640 --> 00:09:03,100
might like objectively,

234
00:09:03,100 --> 00:09:04,980
might only have a 20 to one payoff,

235
00:09:04,980 --> 00:09:07,160
because too many people go and say,

236
00:09:07,160 --> 00:09:09,860
I don't wanna go home not having lost money,

237
00:09:09,860 --> 00:09:11,600
so I'm gonna take my remaining money

238
00:09:11,600 --> 00:09:13,840
and dump it on this long shot horse,

239
00:09:13,840 --> 00:09:17,100
and come on, come on, she always wins,

240
00:09:18,200 --> 00:09:19,040
or whatever.

241
00:09:20,480 --> 00:09:23,640
So that is one that seems pretty well established.

242
00:09:23,640 --> 00:09:27,160
So says that if you do want to improve your odds,

243
00:09:27,160 --> 00:09:28,040
don't bet on long shots,

244
00:09:28,040 --> 00:09:30,440
just go and bet on the safe ones.

245
00:09:30,440 --> 00:09:32,560
And if you feel like that isn't risky enough,

246
00:09:32,560 --> 00:09:35,400
you could just go and bet on more safe horses,

247
00:09:35,400 --> 00:09:37,240
or bet larger stakes.

248
00:09:37,240 --> 00:09:38,360
You could still lose just as much money,

249
00:09:38,360 --> 00:09:39,800
whatever you want, right?

250
00:09:40,800 --> 00:09:41,920
But with better odds.

251
00:09:43,560 --> 00:09:47,360
And then another example is lottery betting,

252
00:09:47,360 --> 00:09:50,880
which also often uses this parimutuel betting system.

253
00:09:50,880 --> 00:09:53,200
So whenever you hear about how the pot

254
00:09:53,200 --> 00:09:55,680
has grown to a certain amount of money,

255
00:09:55,680 --> 00:09:58,400
the pot has grown to $100 million for this lottery.

256
00:09:58,400 --> 00:09:59,900
Well, the normal things that happens

257
00:09:59,900 --> 00:10:01,520
is that they actually start selling

258
00:10:01,520 --> 00:10:03,240
more lottery tickets for those.

259
00:10:03,240 --> 00:10:06,240
And then the more lottery tickets sold,

260
00:10:06,240 --> 00:10:08,400
then eventually the fact there's a huge pot

261
00:10:08,400 --> 00:10:10,280
doesn't mean that in equilibrium,

262
00:10:10,280 --> 00:10:11,680
the tickets are not especially good

263
00:10:11,680 --> 00:10:14,440
because more tickets get sold,

264
00:10:14,440 --> 00:10:17,500
and of course there could be one winner

265
00:10:17,500 --> 00:10:18,340
that gets the whole thing,

266
00:10:18,340 --> 00:10:20,840
but there's way more tickets, so your odds are less.

267
00:10:20,840 --> 00:10:24,720
But also very common with these kinds of lottery systems

268
00:10:24,720 --> 00:10:27,540
is that if people can choose their own numbers,

269
00:10:27,540 --> 00:10:29,320
and people like being able to choose their own numbers,

270
00:10:29,320 --> 00:10:31,040
it gives this feeling of, oh my God,

271
00:10:31,040 --> 00:10:35,280
like I can decide whether I win by choosing my own numbers,

272
00:10:35,280 --> 00:10:36,800
then in systems like this,

273
00:10:36,800 --> 00:10:40,120
what happens if multiple people choose the same numbers?

274
00:10:40,120 --> 00:10:42,280
They have to split the pot.

275
00:10:42,280 --> 00:10:43,640
Now, what's interesting about this

276
00:10:43,640 --> 00:10:45,520
is that there are patterns in numbers

277
00:10:45,520 --> 00:10:47,200
that people voluntarily pick.

278
00:10:47,200 --> 00:10:50,260
People pick a lot of birthdays and other dates.

279
00:10:50,260 --> 00:10:53,400
So if you have the option of picking any number

280
00:10:53,400 --> 00:10:56,320
between like one and 60 on three numbers,

281
00:10:56,320 --> 00:10:58,480
then if you go and pick a first number

282
00:10:58,480 --> 00:11:00,240
that is greater than 12,

283
00:11:00,240 --> 00:11:01,280
and then, let's see,

284
00:11:01,280 --> 00:11:05,800
so then another number that is greater than 31,

285
00:11:07,580 --> 00:11:09,560
and I guess the last one, if you do the year,

286
00:11:09,560 --> 00:11:11,760
then you wanna pick a number that is,

287
00:11:11,760 --> 00:11:13,920
let's see, I guess I'll go, let's see, so.

288
00:11:16,000 --> 00:11:17,820
Well, let's say, I guess you could go and pick a number

289
00:11:17,820 --> 00:11:20,500
that few living people have as their birthday, right?

290
00:11:20,500 --> 00:11:23,600
So right now, there'd be very few people alive

291
00:11:23,600 --> 00:11:25,360
who would be buying lottery tickets

292
00:11:25,360 --> 00:11:30,360
who would have like 18 as the last digit

293
00:11:31,000 --> 00:11:32,760
of their birth year, right?

294
00:11:32,760 --> 00:11:35,400
Those people would be 99 years old.

295
00:11:35,400 --> 00:11:36,680
So you go and you pick numbers

296
00:11:36,680 --> 00:11:38,520
that are normally not picked as birthdays

297
00:11:38,520 --> 00:11:40,720
or for other superstitious reasons,

298
00:11:40,720 --> 00:11:43,000
and in this way, you can greatly reduce your risk

299
00:11:43,000 --> 00:11:44,980
of having to share the pot.

300
00:11:44,980 --> 00:11:47,840
And in this way, it is actually possible

301
00:11:47,840 --> 00:11:49,440
for people to buy lottery tickets

302
00:11:49,440 --> 00:11:51,140
that have an expected positive sum

303
00:11:52,000 --> 00:11:53,560
under very special conditions.

304
00:11:53,560 --> 00:11:58,400
All right, so economists often dismiss lotteries

305
00:11:58,400 --> 00:12:02,640
as a tax on stupidity, but Thaler shows there are cases

306
00:12:02,640 --> 00:12:04,960
where it's at least has a positive return,

307
00:12:04,960 --> 00:12:06,520
which doesn't seem that stupid.

308
00:12:07,660 --> 00:12:09,080
He does also admit that you would have to live

309
00:12:09,080 --> 00:12:10,160
for thousands of years for this

310
00:12:10,160 --> 00:12:12,960
to actually become a viable way of getting rich,

311
00:12:12,960 --> 00:12:14,840
so it's not to me that impressive,

312
00:12:14,840 --> 00:12:17,440
but still given that a lot of people play lotteries,

313
00:12:17,440 --> 00:12:19,280
the fact that people go and pick these losing numbers

314
00:12:19,280 --> 00:12:21,120
so frequently, kind of interesting.

315
00:12:21,120 --> 00:12:26,120
All right, so questions about betting market anomalies.

316
00:12:26,280 --> 00:12:28,920
Wondering about any of these.

317
00:12:28,920 --> 00:12:30,240
All right, so we're not, yeah, yes.

318
00:12:30,240 --> 00:12:32,120
I was wondering if you'd heard of the Martingale

319
00:12:32,120 --> 00:12:34,160
betting system, betting strategy?

320
00:12:34,160 --> 00:12:35,680
Is that the one where you keep doubling your bet

321
00:12:35,680 --> 00:12:36,520
and telling them to make money?

322
00:12:36,520 --> 00:12:37,360
Yes, yes.

323
00:12:37,360 --> 00:12:39,760
Do people ever try it, and then at what point

324
00:12:39,760 --> 00:12:40,800
does that end up breaking down?

325
00:12:40,800 --> 00:12:45,160
Right, yeah, so the strategy of going and betting on roulette

326
00:12:45,160 --> 00:12:47,160
and every time you lose, doubling your bet.

327
00:12:47,160 --> 00:12:48,280
From the point of view of casinos,

328
00:12:48,280 --> 00:12:49,840
this is just like a new sucker showed up

329
00:12:49,840 --> 00:12:52,160
and did a bet twice as large as the last sucker.

330
00:12:52,160 --> 00:12:53,840
They're perfectly happy for it.

331
00:12:53,840 --> 00:12:56,040
Of course, if you literally had infinite money,

332
00:12:56,040 --> 00:12:59,160
then this could work, but how many people on earth

333
00:12:59,160 --> 00:13:00,240
have infinite money?

334
00:13:00,240 --> 00:13:01,080
Zero.

335
00:13:01,080 --> 00:13:04,200
No one has infinite money, so it actually doesn't work.

336
00:13:04,200 --> 00:13:07,600
Yes, so it's one, of course, what you could say

337
00:13:07,600 --> 00:13:10,280
is that the median, if you have a lot of money,

338
00:13:10,280 --> 00:13:14,200
the median is good, but the mean is still terrible.

339
00:13:14,200 --> 00:13:15,160
Yeah, yeah, Ash?

340
00:13:15,160 --> 00:13:17,600
This is actually why casinos have minimal bets, though,

341
00:13:17,600 --> 00:13:19,880
because they start with just betting one penny.

342
00:13:19,880 --> 00:13:24,280
Yeah, well, I mean, it would make sense

343
00:13:24,280 --> 00:13:26,760
to have a minimum bet anyway, because it's not worth their

344
00:13:26,760 --> 00:13:28,080
time to handle a bet of a penny.

345
00:13:28,080 --> 00:13:30,720
But yeah, Lane?

346
00:13:30,720 --> 00:13:33,560
I'm probably good at casinos too much.

347
00:13:33,560 --> 00:13:36,280
They've always had minimum bets, but the end result

348
00:13:36,280 --> 00:13:39,320
of a betting system that presses on loss, like the Martingale,

349
00:13:39,320 --> 00:13:41,520
is that the expected value can't change, right?

350
00:13:41,520 --> 00:13:43,240
There's no change in the betting system,

351
00:13:43,240 --> 00:13:46,240
but you have more small wins and a few

352
00:13:46,240 --> 00:13:47,640
number of catastrophic losses.

353
00:13:47,640 --> 00:13:48,160
Yes, yes.

354
00:13:48,160 --> 00:13:51,640
Similarly, if you press on a win instead, it flips it.

355
00:13:51,640 --> 00:13:52,600
Yes, yes.

356
00:13:52,600 --> 00:13:53,240
That's right.

357
00:13:53,240 --> 00:13:54,760
Anyways, but I'm sorry, sorry, sorry,

